# PREPARED, NOT APPLIED. This is not a workflow file and GitHub does not read it:
# the extension is .fragment so that dropping it in this directory cannot start
# anything. It replaces three steps in .github/workflows/record.yml and leaves the
# rest of that file alone. See scripts/s3-archive/RUNBOOK.md step 4.4.
#
# WHAT COMES OUT, roughly 80 lines:
#   "Check out the recordings branch into a worktree"   the whole orphan/worktree dance
#   "Commit the round"                                  git add, the base rev, the message
#   "Push, retrying with rebase"                        the three-attempt rebase --onto loop
#
# All three exist to append blobs to a git branch. S3 objects are independent, so
# there is no conflict to rebase around and none of it has an equivalent.
#
# WHAT MUST NOT CHANGE, and this is the part to check when applying it:
#
#   1. `-out` still points at a plain directory. keel record writes files; where
#      they end up afterwards is this workflow's business and not the recorder's.
#   2. The recorder still exits 0 when Horizon is busy. A tick that was written is
#      a success no matter what status it recorded, and a recorder whose red gets
#      ignored is worse than one that is never red.
#   3. concurrency cancel-in-progress stays FALSE. A run in flight holds a reading
#      of a ledger that is closing now.
#   4. The manifest commit is what keeps this an archive of EVIDENCE rather than an
#      archive of bytes. If it is dropped to save a step, the move is not worth
#      making at all.
#
# Substitute: <ACCOUNT_ID>, <ROLE_NAME>, <REGION>, <BUCKET>.

# --- replaces `permissions:` at the top of the file -------------------------
#
# id-token: write is what lets the job ask GitHub for the OIDC JWT. contents:
# write is STILL needed, because the manifest is still committed to the orphan
# branch; only the gzipped blobs stop being committed.
permissions:
  contents: write
  id-token: write

# --- replaces the three steps named above -----------------------------------

      - name: Assume the recorder role
        uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
        with:
          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:role/<ROLE_NAME>
          role-session-name: keel-recorder-${{ github.run_id }}
          aws-region: <REGION>

      # sync and not cp --recursive: sync skips a key that is already there, so a
      # re-run after a partial failure uploads only what is missing. Every key is
      # new anyway, because keel record never reuses a name, but a step that is
      # safe to re-run is a step somebody will actually re-run.
      - name: Upload the round
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          aws s3 sync out/recordings "s3://<BUCKET>/recordings/" \
            --only-show-errors --no-progress

      # The manifest is written from the LOCAL files, before anything is fetched
      # back. Hashing what was uploaded by downloading it again would prove only
      # that S3 returns what S3 stored, which nobody doubts. Hashing the bytes the
      # recorder produced is what makes the manifest a claim about the recording.
      - name: Write the sha256 manifest
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          day="$(date -u +'%Y-%m-%d')"
          bash scripts/s3-archive/manifest.sh out/recordings \
            "archive/recordings/manifest/${day}.sha256"

      # The orphan branch survives, holding text instead of blobs. It grows at
      # roughly a twenty-fourth of the old rate, and it is the chain that lets a
      # stranger check the bucket without trusting anyone.
      - name: Commit the manifest
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          cd archive
          git add -A recordings/manifest
          git diff --cached --quiet && exit 0
          stamp="$(date -u +'%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')"
          git commit -m "Manifest for ${{ steps.record.outputs.ticks_written }} ticks at ${stamp}" \
                     -m "Objects are in s3://<BUCKET>/recordings/. Verify with scripts/s3-archive/verify-manifest.sh."
          git push origin "$RECORDINGS_BRANCH"

# --- one line for .gitignore ------------------------------------------------
#
# /recordings/* is excluded with a negation for samples/. The manifest directory
# needs the same treatment or git will ignore the very file this design depends on,
# which is the exact failure .gitignore already records once: 10-validation.md named
# recordings/samples/ while .gitignore re-included recordings/sample/, and evidence
# put where the methodology said to put it was silently dropped.
#
#   !/recordings/manifest/
